s ::= import "./schema.zt";
h ::= import "./helpers.zt";

area :: s.Area = (h.area "hardware" "Hardware and network targets: Zenoh, lidar, ROS 2, boards" {
    "components/applications/**";
    "components/services/network*/**";
    "components/services/zenoh*/**";
    "contracts/zenoh-*/**";
    "contracts/network-*/**";
    "contracts/rpi5-ros2-demo/**";
  } {
    -- legacy 01a0f5fa-f2c4-71cf-962b-c62cbea2ef68 (open): Zenoh Profile 0 transport for the R0 two-node exchange
    -- R0 (the legacy milestone 01a00b4d-2400-7987-93d6-19201c397dff) needs two ROS 2 node components to
    -- exchange the declared bounded topic over a minimal Zenoh Profile 0 session under AArch64 QEMU. The
    -- decoder, the contracts/zenoh-profile/v1 vocabulary and the frozen contracts/rpi5-ros2-demo/v2
    -- fixture exist; nothing yet encodes a Zenoh message, runs a session, serializes CDR, owns the TCP
    -- link, runs as a node, is granted its authority by a composition, or is observed exchanging the
    -- topic. R0 is a pre-cutoff prose item and cannot carry a devloop record, so its remaining work needs
    -- an owner that can.
    (h.planned "01a1b49c-aaf1-746a-ab26-ec947c8a0c4d"
      "Two target-qualified node components exchange the four declared slime_demo_msgs/msg/Counter samples through a Zenoh Profile 0 session over one TCP link under AArch64 QEMU, with exact generation-declared graph and stream authority, no router, gossip, scouting or discovery authority anywhere in the graph, explicit denial of unsupported transport features, and finite resource bounds. Every host slice beneath it passes, so the exchange cannot rest on a codec, session or runtime that no gate observed."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 transport for the R0 two-node exchange"
      "rehomed from a open item"
      {
        h.observedWhere "exchange-observed" "rpi5_ros2_zenoh_check" h.noPredicates "The recipe runs the composition judge, the encoder, session, CDR and transport recipes, then boots the sel4-zenoh image and judges the four reported batches byte for byte against a host wire reference that is itself proved against the upstream-encoded corpus. It exits non-zero while any slice is missing, so a host-only run cannot satisfy it: R0's exit condition is a QEMU exchange. The recipe's exit code is reported by the gate adapter, not by the checker.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a0f5fa-f2c4-71cf-962b-c62cbea2ef68"; }; };
    -- legacy 01a0f5fa-f704-73e0-b3a1-cd2debc429e9 (done): Zenoh Profile 0 encoder reproducing the upstream golden bytes
    -- components/lib/src/zenoh_profile0.rs decodes the Zenoh Profile 0 wire subset and nothing encodes it.
    -- A publisher or subscriber component cannot open a session, declare a subscriber or send a Put
    -- without bytes that an eclipse-zenoh 1.0.0 peer accepts, and a hand-written encoder that merely
    -- round-trips with this repository's own decoder would agree with the decoder's mistakes.
    (h.req "01a1b49c-aaff-771c-bc75-f8f5be7b4b96"
      "For every accepted batch in the decoder corpus the encoder, given the values the corpus summary names, produces exactly the corpus bytes. Each case is reported by name on the test transcript as an encode ok line carrying the produced hex, so the checker compares bytes it read rather than a count the test asserts."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 encoder reproducing the upstream golden bytes"
      {
        h.observedWhere "golden-bytes-reproduced" "zenoh_encoder_check" { h.count "casesObserved" 17; } "The corpus holds 17 accepted batches and 17 is the number the corpus makes, not a lower bound: the checker requires every corpus name exactly once and compares each produced hex with the upstream bytes, so a missing batch, an invented one or a changed byte fails before the count is reported.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a0f5fa-f704-73e0-b3a1-cd2debc429e9"; }; };
    (h.req "01a1b49c-aaff-771c-bc75-f8f64943002e"
      "The encoder refuses, rather than truncates, a key expression over 129 bytes, an attachment that is not 33 bytes, a payload over 12 bytes, a cookie over 64 bytes, an empty or over-16-byte ZID, a wildcard key expression, and a batch whose encoding would exceed 512 bytes. Each refusal is reported as an encode refused line naming its control."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 encoder reproducing the upstream golden bytes"
      {
        h.observedWhere "refusals-observed" "zenoh_encoder_check" { h.count "negativeControlsRefused" 7; } "Seven is the number of encoder controls this specification names; the checker requires exactly those names, each once. Reporting the count is the checker's, not the test's, and the bound is exact so a dropped control is caught.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a0f5fa-f704-73e0-b3a1-cd2debc429e9"; }; };
    (h.req "01a1b49c-aaff-771c-bc75-f8f76d68e79b"
      "The checker's own judge refuses a transcript with a dropped, duplicated, unknown or corrupted line, so a passing run is evidence of byte agreement and not of a quiet transcript."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 encoder reproducing the upstream golden bytes"
      {
        h.observedWhere "encoder-passed" "zenoh_encoder_check" h.noPredicates "The recipe's exit code, reported by the gate adapter rather than by the checker. The checker's controls run first and refuse eight corrupted transcripts, so a judge that accepts anything cannot pass.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a0f5fa-f704-73e0-b3a1-cd2debc429e9"; }; };
    -- legacy 01a0f5fa-fb06-7cbd-9a39-1539785433b7 (done): Zenoh Profile 0 session state machine over the bounded link
    -- Nothing runs a Zenoh Profile 0 session. Opening, keeping alive and closing one link, declaring a
    -- subscriber, delivering a Put exactly once and detecting a replay all depend on state that is neither
    -- in the decoder nor the encoder, and an implementation tangled with the TCP link and a clock could
    -- not be judged without booting QEMU.
    (h.req "01a1b49c-ab0c-7760-a562-e9522e333a1c"
      "The machine completes connect-handshake, listen-handshake, declare-then-push-delivered, teardown-undeclare-then-close and lease-expiry-closes, each reported as a session ok line. Delivery requires a matching prior declaration, and teardown undeclares before it closes, as the demo contract's teardown field states."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 session state machine over the bounded link"
      {
        h.observedWhere "scenarios-observed" "zenoh_session_check" { h.count "casesObserved" 5; } "Five is the number of scenarios this specification names and the checker requires exactly those names once each, so five is not a lower bound. The test, not the checker, drives the machine; the checker only accepts a scenario it was told completed.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a0f5fa-fb06-7cbd-9a39-1539785433b7"; }; };
    (h.req "01a1b49c-ab0c-7760-a562-e953039f9df0"
      "The machine refuses a router peer, a version mismatch, a wildcard key in a declaration, a Put to an undeclared key, a replayed sequence number, a frame before OPEN and a cookie that does not match its INIT, each reported as a session refused line, and each leaves the session either unchanged or closed, never half-open."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 session state machine over the bounded link"
      {
        h.observedWhere "refusals-observed" "zenoh_session_check" { h.count "negativeControlsRefused" 7; } "Seven is the number of refusal controls named here, each required exactly once by name.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a0f5fa-fb06-7cbd-9a39-1539785433b7"; }; };
    (h.req "01a1b49c-ab0c-7760-a562-e9542249c268"
      "The checker's judge refuses a transcript with a dropped, duplicated, unknown or corrupted line."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 session state machine over the bounded link"
      {
        h.observedWhere "session-passed" "zenoh_session_check" h.noPredicates "The recipe's exit code, reported by the gate adapter.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a0f5fa-fb06-7cbd-9a39-1539785433b7"; }; };
    -- legacy 01a0f5fa-ff13-7667-8c59-6f4c6d312535 (done): Classic-CDR codec for the Counter demo message
    -- The demo's payload is classic CDR for slime_demo_msgs/msg/Counter, and no component serializes or
    -- parses it. The frozen contracts/rpi5-ros2-demo/v2 fixture records the exact bytes of four samples,
    -- but its checker derives them in Python; no Rust code produces them, so a node could publish a
    -- payload that no rmw_zenoh peer would decode.
    (h.req "01a1b49c-ab18-72ea-a61a-ab8ef939bfcf"
      "Serializing each of the four fixture samples yields exactly its cdrHex, and parsing that hex yields the same sequence and value. Each is reported as a cdr ok line carrying the sequence, the value and the produced hex."
      "Classic-CDR codec for the Counter demo message"
      {
        h.observedWhere "samples-reproduced" "zenoh_cdr_check" { h.count "casesObserved" 4; } "Four is the number of samples the demo fixture declares (publishCount = 4) and the checker requires sequences 0 to 3 exactly once each, comparing both the contract's hex and bytes it packs itself from the field values, so a fixture edited to match a wrong encoder still fails.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a0f5fa-ff13-7667-8c59-6f4c6d312535"; }; };
    (h.req "01a1b49c-ab19-748f-9c1a-65e5dcde054f"
      "The decoder refuses a truncated header, a truncated body, trailing bytes, a big-endian encapsulation, an unknown encapsulation, nonzero options and an input over the 12-byte maximum serialized size, each reported as a cdr refused line, before reading a field."
      "Classic-CDR codec for the Counter demo message"
      {
        h.observedWhere "refusals-observed" "zenoh_cdr_check" { h.count "negativeControlsRefused" 7; } "Seven is the number of malformed-input controls named here, each required exactly once by name.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a0f5fa-ff13-7667-8c59-6f4c6d312535"; }; };
    (h.req "01a1b49c-ab19-748f-9c1a-65e6a37652a9"
      "The checker's judge refuses a transcript with a dropped, duplicated, unknown or corrupted line, and recomputes each expected sample from its field values rather than trusting the fixture's hex alone."
      "Classic-CDR codec for the Counter demo message"
      {
        h.observedWhere "cdr-passed" "zenoh_cdr_check" h.noPredicates "The recipe's exit code, reported by the gate adapter.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a0f5fa-ff13-7667-8c59-6f4c6d312535"; }; };
    -- legacy 01a0f5fb-0344-7bb5-9d5b-2b79bc0339a8 (done): Zenoh Profile 0 transport runtime over a bounded byte link
    -- A session machine is pure; something must own the TCP link. The transport runtime reads and writes
    -- bytes through the network service's typed adapter (components/lib/src/network_io.rs), reassembles
    -- length-prefixed batches that arrive split or coalesced, waits on the declared notifications instead
    -- of spinning, and must stay inside fixed buffers when the peer is slow, silent, hostile or gone.
    -- Nothing yet connects the session machine to a byte stream, and a runtime written against the real
    -- network service could only be judged by booting QEMU.
    (h.req "01a1b49c-ab25-71a7-92d2-de1e2c63ec79"
      "The runtime completes connector-opens-and-publishes, listener-accepts-and-delivers, split-and-coalesced-batches, backpressure-resumes, peer-close-ends-session and restart-uses-fresh-session over the scripted link, each reported as a link ok line. Restart opens a session with a fresh identity and cannot deliver a sample from the predecessor."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 transport runtime over a bounded byte link"
      {
        h.observedWhere "scenarios-observed" "zenoh_transport_check" { h.count "casesObserved" 6; } "Six is the number of scenarios this specification names and the checker requires exactly those names once each. The recipe's Rust filter is zenoh_link::, so a runtime placed under another module path runs no test and fails with no passing result rather than reporting zero.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a0f5fb-0344-7bb5-9d5b-2b79bc0339a8"; }; };
    (h.req "01a1b49c-ab25-71a7-92d2-de1fb372c3bf"
      "The runtime refuses an oversized length prefix, a zero length prefix, garbage after OPEN, a receive that would exceed its queue, a send past the retry limit, a send after close and a handle from a closed session, each reported as a link refused line, and each leaves its buffers within their declared bounds."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 transport runtime over a bounded byte link"
      {
        h.observedWhere "refusals-observed" "zenoh_transport_check" { h.count "negativeControlsRefused" 7; } "Seven is the number of link refusal controls named here, each required exactly once by name.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a0f5fb-0344-7bb5-9d5b-2b79bc0339a8"; }; };
    (h.req "01a1b49c-ab25-71a7-92d2-de20e7481cde"
      "The checker's judge refuses a transcript with a dropped, duplicated, unknown or corrupted line."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 transport runtime over a bounded byte link"
      {
        h.observedWhere "transport-passed" "zenoh_transport_check" h.noPredicates "The recipe's exit code, reported by the gate adapter.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a0f5fb-0344-7bb5-9d5b-2b79bc0339a8"; }; };
    -- legacy 01a0f5fb-0780-754f-b2f4-e7513c86b63c (done): Publisher and subscriber nodes with the sel4-zenoh composition
    -- Two components must act as the demo's publisher and subscriber and be granted exactly what the demo
    -- declares. No composition runs them, so there is no generation in which the declared connect
    -- endpoint, the single admitted loopback listener and the absence of router, scouting, wildcard and
    -- DNS authority can be read and judged. Their authority is a property of the system spec and the
    -- manifest derived from it, not of the component bytes.
    (h.req "01a1b49c-ab33-7247-9e7b-6b4527d0bd95"
      "The derived sel4-zenoh composition has generation 168 and exactly the instances init, network-service, ros2-demo-publisher and ros2-demo-subscriber. It declares one network destination, held by the publisher, exact IPv4 TCP to 127.0.0.1:7447 with exactly connect, send and recv, no listener limit and no DNS records; it declares exactly two applications, a rightless loopback client and a loopback listener bound to 127.0.0.1:7447 holding exactly listen, send and recv and admitting exactly the publisher holder."
      "Publisher and subscriber nodes with the sel4-zenoh composition"
      {
        h.observedWhere "authority-judged" "zenoh_composition_check" { h.count "casesObserved" 15; h.count "negativeControlsRefused" 15; } "Fifteen is the number of authority facts the judge verifies on the derived manifest: generation, instance set, destination count, holder, endpoint, transport, rights, listen and resolver limits, application set, publisher role, listener role, listener bind, wildcard refusal, admitted peer and backend. The judge is a pure function of rows, run first on an honest synthetic composition and on its mutations, then on the manifest, so the count is what the declared composition makes.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a0f5fb-0780-754f-b2f4-e7513c86b63c"; }; };
    (h.req "01a1b49c-ab33-7247-9e7b-6b4609c654e1"
      "The composition judge refuses every one of its 15 mutations of an honest composition, so a passing run is evidence of the declared authority and not of a judge that accepts any rows."
      "Publisher and subscriber nodes with the sel4-zenoh composition"
      {
        h.observedWhere "composition-passed" "zenoh_composition_check" h.noPredicates "The recipe's exit code, reported by the gate adapter. The recipe fails while the composition does not exist, so a missing manifest cannot pass.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a0f5fb-0780-754f-b2f4-e7513c86b63c"; }; };
    -- legacy 01a0fc73-3bcd-75c7-9ec5-0e9f84c7505b (done): QEMU exchange gate for the Zenoh Profile 0 demo
    -- R0's exit condition is an exchange under AArch64 QEMU, and the existing network planes judge byte
    -- counts and markers but nothing judges Zenoh bytes. A plane that only matched the nodes' success
    -- marker would pass if the nodes agreed with each other on wrong bytes, so the claim would rest on the
    -- implementation grading itself.
    (h.req "01a1b49c-ab64-70df-86ef-cf43ceea97f1"
      "Under AArch64 QEMU the two nodes exchange the four declared samples over one session, and each reported batch equals the host reference's encoding of its own decoded fields, carries the demo key expression and CDR payload, and is received byte for byte as sent. The subscriber validates values 10, 20, 30 and 40 for sequences 0 to 3, undeclares before it closes, and the markers close the graph healthy with four required instances completed."
      "QEMU exchange gate for the Zenoh Profile 0 demo"
      {
        h.observedWhere "exchange-judged" "rpi5_ros2_zenoh_check" { h.count "casesObserved" 4; h.count "bytesObserved" 736; } "Four samples and 736 wire bytes are what the fixture makes, not floors: each frame is 184 bytes (a 129-byte key expression, the 33-byte attachment, a 12-byte payload and 9 bytes of framing), so 736 is four frames exactly and a changed key, attachment or payload size changes it. The count is of bytes the host reference decoded from the guest's reports, not a value the checker assigns.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a0fc73-3bcd-75c7-9ec5-0e9f84c7505b"; }; };
    (h.req "01a1b49c-ab64-70df-86ef-cf441da51f59"
      "The publisher is refused an undeclared endpoint, a listen and a scouting attempt, and the subscriber is refused a connect, each a real request to the network service that is checked to be denied before it is reported, once by name, during the node's setup. The scouting attempt is a well-formed UDP connect to Zenoh's multicast scouting group, which no destination row names."
      "QEMU exchange gate for the Zenoh Profile 0 demo"
      {
        h.observedWhere "exchange-judged" "rpi5_ros2_zenoh_check" { h.count "casesObserved" 4; h.count "bytesObserved" 736; } "Four samples and 736 wire bytes are what the fixture makes, not floors: each frame is 184 bytes (a 129-byte key expression, the 33-byte attachment, a 12-byte payload and 9 bytes of framing), so 736 is four frames exactly and a changed key, attachment or payload size changes it. The count is of bytes the host reference decoded from the guest's reports, not a value the checker assigns.";
        h.observedWhere "qemu-passed" "rpi5_ros2_zenoh_check" h.noPredicates "The recipe's exit code, reported by the gate adapter. The recipe depends on the composition and the four host recipes, so an exchange cannot pass while any slice beneath it fails.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a0fc73-3bcd-75c7-9ec5-0e9f84c7505b"; }; };
    (h.req "01a1b49c-ab64-70df-86ef-cf45f82e23d6"
      "The transcript judge refuses all 28 mutations of an honest transcript before it judges the guest's."
      "QEMU exchange gate for the Zenoh Profile 0 demo"
      {
        h.observedWhere "controls-refused" "rpi5_ros2_zenoh_check" { h.count "negativeControlsRefused" 43; } "Forty-three is 28 transcript mutations plus 15 composition mutations, each refused before the guest's evidence is judged. The bound is exact so a dropped control is caught.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a0fc73-3bcd-75c7-9ec5-0e9f84c7505b"; }; };
    -- legacy 01a11686-e2e7-72b5-a46a-6bca4cca7e15 (done): Zenoh Profile 0 codec and session agreeing with the upstream eclipse-zenoh 1.0.0 crates
    -- The Zenoh Profile 0 encoder, decoder and session machine are judged on the host against 17 batches
    -- that the upstream eclipse-zenoh 1.0.0 encoder produced once and that were then copied into this
    -- repository. Nothing re-runs the upstream codec against ours, so a disagreement outside those 17
    -- batches is invisible. One existed: upstream holds a Zenoh ID as a little-endian integer, drops its
    -- trailing zero bytes and refuses zero, and our encoder wrote such an ID at its given length. It was
    -- found by a harness kept outside the repository, whose result cannot be reproduced from a clone.
    (h.req "01a1b49c-aba0-7213-a89b-a8bbc2f6bffa"
      "The harness reproduces the 17 accepted corpus batches from upstream's own encoder, and over a 6000-case sweep our encoder equals upstream's byte for byte (5993 compared), our decoder accepts everything upstream encodes, upstream reads and re-encodes everything ours encodes, and one 11-batch session is read back identically. The 7 sweep cases that Profile 0 declines are refused for one reason only, a non-canonical Zenoh ID."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 codec and session agreeing with the upstream eclipse-zenoh 1.0.0 crates"
      {
        h.observedWhere "cases-observed" "zenoh_xcheck_check" { h.count "casesObserved" 6036; } "The checker counts what it matched: 17 corpus batches, 6000 sweep cases, 11 session batches and 8 Zenoh ID probes, 6036 in all. The count is exact rather than a floor: fewer means the harness skipped work, and more means it compared something the exam does not name. The checker also requires the zero differences, the one refusal reason and the per-ID outcomes before it reports the count, so the number is only reported for a run that agreed.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a11686-e2e7-72b5-a46a-6bca4cca7e15"; }; };
    (h.req "01a1b49c-aba0-7213-a89b-a8bcb9359c0e"
      "A Zenoh ID that is all zero or ends in a zero byte is refused by our encoder and decoder, as upstream's integer representation requires, and any other ID of one to sixteen bytes round-trips."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 codec and session agreeing with the upstream eclipse-zenoh 1.0.0 crates"
      {
        h.observedWhere "cases-observed" "zenoh_xcheck_check" { h.count "casesObserved" 6036; } "The checker counts what it matched: 17 corpus batches, 6000 sweep cases, 11 session batches and 8 Zenoh ID probes, 6036 in all. The count is exact rather than a floor: fewer means the harness skipped work, and more means it compared something the exam does not name. The checker also requires the zero differences, the one refusal reason and the per-ID outcomes before it reports the count, so the number is only reported for a run that agreed.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a11686-e2e7-72b5-a46a-6bca4cca7e15"; }; };
    (h.req "01a1b49c-aba0-7213-a89b-a8bd18de4def"
      "The judge refuses a harness that reports fewer cases, a difference, an unexplained refusal, a dropped or duplicated line, a corrupted corpus byte, or a Zenoh ID outcome that contradicts the ID's bytes, so a passing run is evidence of agreement and not of a quiet harness."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 codec and session agreeing with the upstream eclipse-zenoh 1.0.0 crates"
      {
        h.observedWhere "controls-refused" "zenoh_xcheck_check" { h.atLeast "negativeControlsRefused" 17; h.atMost "negativeControlsRefused" 128; } "The checker runs 17 mutations of an honest transcript through its own judge before it runs the harness, and reports how many it refused. The bound is inclusive at 17 so a checker that drops a control is caught; the upper bound is a sanity limit, not a target.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a11686-e2e7-72b5-a46a-6bca4cca7e15"; }; };
    (h.req "01a1b49c-aba0-7213-a89b-a8bee474fe05"
      "zenoh_xcheck_check passes as the gate adapter observes it."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 codec and session agreeing with the upstream eclipse-zenoh 1.0.0 crates"
      {
        h.observedWhere "recipe-passed" "zenoh_xcheck_check" h.noPredicates "The recipe's exit code, reported by the gate adapter rather than by the checker, so a checker cannot vouch for itself.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a11686-e2e7-72b5-a46a-6bca4cca7e15"; }; };
    -- legacy 01a12f13-a343-7924-af20-18148fc1b0d9 (done): Zenoh Profile 0 session interoperating with a stock eclipse-zenoh 1.0 peer on the demo topic
    -- The Zenoh Profile 0 session and decoder refuse the first message a stock eclipse-zenoh 1.0 peer
    -- sends, so the bounded profile cannot talk to a Zenoh peer it did not write. A capture of a stock
    -- eclipse-zenoh 1.0.0 peer, not a model of one, shows what the profile lacks: the peer attaches an
    -- optional QoS unit extension to its INIT_SYN and a per-message QoS extension to every OAM and to the
    -- outer DECLARE that wraps each DeclareKeyExpr and DeclareSubscriber inside a FRAME (its Puts, and the
    -- DECLARE around its DeclareFinal, carry none), announces itself with an OAM and a DeclareFinal,
    -- registers the demo key as a numeric alias before it declares or publishes on it, and sends a one-
    -- byte KEEP_ALIVE every quarter of its lease. It closes a link that has heard nothing from the other
    -- side for that lease. Profile 0 refuses each of these, so of the 19 batches the peer sent while
    -- connecting or listening, 5 are accepted, and a session that did accept them would still be closed by
    -- the peer unless it also sent keep-alives of its own. Nothing in the repository holds those batches,
    -- so the gap and the fix are both unchecked.
    (h.req "01a1b49c-abf1-7139-b388-c6efe3f28206"
      "Every batch the captured stock peer sent while it connected to a Profile 0 listener or listened for a Profile 0 connector is taken by the session or the link and raises exactly the notices the exchange needs: 19 captured batches, the four demo samples delivered with their values, sequence numbers and timestamps, the attachment the decoder reads from each captured push (sequence, timestamp and the 16-byte GID) equal to the captured one, and the peer's CLOSE ending the session."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 session interoperating with a stock eclipse-zenoh 1.0 peer on the demo topic"
      {
        h.observedWhere "cases-observed" "zenoh_stock_check" { h.count "casesObserved" 69; } "The checker counts what it matched: 12 stock scenarios, 19 replayed captured batches and the demo's 4 delivered samples, 35 positive cases, and 34 refusals, 69 in all. The count is exact rather than a floor: fewer means the Rust tests skipped a scenario, more means they reported something the exam does not name. The checker also requires every refusal class, the demo payloads and the exact scenario names before it reports the count, so the number is only reported for a run that agreed.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a12f13-a343-7924-af20-18148fc1b0d9"; }; };
    (h.req "01a1b49c-abf1-7139-b388-c6f0b33f3f13"
      "An open session sends a KEEP_ALIVE at least as often as the captured peer sends its own, once every quarter of the announced lease, whenever it has sent nothing else; accepts the peer's KEEP_ALIVE as traffic; and closes with the expiry reason when it has heard nothing for that lease. The captured peer fixes the numbers: it sent a keep-alive every quarter of its lease, at 2000 ms and at 10000 ms, closed a link that heard nothing from us for one lease (2.002 s after our last transmission on a 2 s lease, 10.002 s on a 10 s lease), and stayed connected for the whole run while we sent one every 700 ms on a 2 s lease. Whether a slower cadence also keeps it open was not measured."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 session interoperating with a stock eclipse-zenoh 1.0 peer on the demo topic"
      {
        h.observedWhere "cases-observed" "zenoh_stock_check" { h.count "casesObserved" 69; } "The checker counts what it matched: 12 stock scenarios, 19 replayed captured batches and the demo's 4 delivered samples, 35 positive cases, and 34 refusals, 69 in all. The count is exact rather than a floor: fewer means the Rust tests skipped a scenario, more means they reported something the exam does not name. The checker also requires every refusal class, the demo payloads and the exact scenario names before it reports the count, so the number is only reported for a run that agreed.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a12f13-a343-7924-af20-18148fc1b0d9"; }; };
    (h.req "01a1b49c-abf1-7139-b388-c6f13d1482ee"
      "A key-expression alias is declared once, resolves a Put or a declaration to the declared key and no other, and is refused as invalid-keyexpr when undeclared, refused as over-bound when a second is declared, and refused as invalid-keyexpr when redeclared to a different key or when the same declaration is repeated, since it is declared once and a repeat is not a no-op. The key of a DeclareKeyExpr keeps the existing 129-byte bound: a 130-byte alias key, the captured declaration with one byte added, is refused as over-bound. The alias resolves to the declared key and no other: a suffix after alias 1, on a subscriber declaration and on a Put, is refused as invalid-keyexpr, not accepted, concatenated or ignored. The alias resolves a DeclareSubscriber and a Put only: an UndeclareSubscriber naming scope 1 is refused as invalid-keyexpr. The session holds one subscriber: a second declaration received from the peer, and a second one this side sends, are each refused as subscriber-limit and leave the first in place; an undeclaration that does not name the held subscriber (another id, another key, or none held), received or sent, is refused as unknown-subscriber and leaves the held one in place. It reports at most four events from one FRAME: a FRAME of five valid aliased PUSHes is refused as too-many-messages and none is dropped silently. A KEEP_ALIVE is traffic only on an open session: in each of the four states before OPEN (idle, awaiting INIT_ACK, awaiting OPEN_SYN, awaiting OPEN_ACK) it is refused as unexpected-message and the session is unchanged. A tick sends nothing before OPEN: in the idle state and in each of the three awaiting states, a tick between a quarter lease and the lease produces no output and changes no state. When the link has sent an ordinary batch, or still holds one pending, at a quarter-lease boundary, no KEEP_ALIVE is added: the batch goes out whole, the cadence restarts from it and the link stays open. A KEEP_ALIVE is exactly the one byte 04: a batch that adds a byte to it is refused as trailing-bytes, as a trailing byte on a CLOSE or an INIT_SYN is. The 19 captured batches are replayed in capture order, all of the connecting peer's and then all of the listening peer's, and a transcript that swaps or interleaves the two groups is refused."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 session interoperating with a stock eclipse-zenoh 1.0 peer on the demo topic"
      {
        h.observedWhere "cases-observed" "zenoh_stock_check" { h.count "casesObserved" 69; } "The checker counts what it matched: 12 stock scenarios, 19 replayed captured batches and the demo's 4 delivered samples, 35 positive cases, and 34 refusals, 69 in all. The count is exact rather than a floor: fewer means the Rust tests skipped a scenario, more means they reported something the exam does not name. The checker also requires every refusal class, the demo payloads and the exact scenario names before it reports the count, so the number is only reported for a run that agreed.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a12f13-a343-7924-af20-18148fc1b0d9"; }; };
    (h.req "01a1b49c-abf1-7139-b388-c6f26fe240c7"
      "Everything the profile exists to keep out is still refused with its class after the stock peer is accepted, and the checker, not the implementation, holds the refusal corpus: the 101 refused vectors in components/lib/src/zenoh_profile0/vectors.txt other than transport_keep_alive keep their name, class and bytes, and transport_keep_alive, while it exists, keeps the single captured byte 04 and its class unsupported-message while it stays refused, and the 17 accepted batches keep their name, bytes and summary, and the 14 stream-framing rows (ten accepted, four refused: the 512-byte stream bound, a zero length, a refusal after a valid batch, and the reassembly cases) keep their name, verdict, class, chunks and summary, so none can be replaced, dropped, renamed or duplicated. The decoder is held to those rows by running the existing corpus test, zenoh_profile0::tests::corpus_agrees, in the same gate, so a decoder change cannot hide behind an unchanged corpus file. The decoder's existing property tests (every prefix of an accepted batch, single-bit damage, every two-chunk split) walk only the accepted rows of that file and so never see a stock batch; the stock tests therefore run the same three properties on each of the 19 captured batches and print one property line for each, in capture order, which the checker requires. No row is added to the corpus for this, because the encoder gate and the cross-check harness read every accepted row. Specifically: a client and a router INIT_SYN as unsupported-whatami, a queryable declaration and a request as unsupported-message, a wildcard key as wildcard-keyexpr, any other extension, either QoS extension marked mandatory, and the optional per-message QoS extension on a FRAME header, a PUSH, a Put, the inner header of a DeclareSubscriber body (the capture has it on the outer DECLARE around that body, not on the inner header), the outer DECLARE around a DeclareFinal, or an UndeclareSubscriber, where no capture has one, as unsupported-extension (the six batches are captured ones, or for the undeclaration the accepted corpus row frame_undeclare_subscriber_sn7, plus the two-byte extension, which the upstream decoder reads). Extensions on INIT_ACK, OPEN_SYN and OPEN_ACK were not seen on a stock peer, so none is accepted there."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 session interoperating with a stock eclipse-zenoh 1.0 peer on the demo topic"
      {
        h.observedWhere "cases-observed" "zenoh_stock_check" { h.count "casesObserved" 69; } "The checker counts what it matched: 12 stock scenarios, 19 replayed captured batches and the demo's 4 delivered samples, 35 positive cases, and 34 refusals, 69 in all. The count is exact rather than a floor: fewer means the Rust tests skipped a scenario, more means they reported something the exam does not name. The checker also requires every refusal class, the demo payloads and the exact scenario names before it reports the count, so the number is only reported for a run that agreed.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a12f13-a343-7924-af20-18148fc1b0d9"; }; };
    (h.req "01a1b49c-abf1-7139-b388-c6f3ed52110a"
      "The judge refuses a transcript that drops, duplicates, reorders or invents a scenario, replay or delivered sample, or reports a line of a recognised kind that is incomplete or has extra fields, or any record that is not a stock line, reports a refusal with the wrong class or none, accepts a stock client or router, or lacks the keep-alive or lease scenarios, and refuses a capture whose pushes differ from the demo's four samples and attachments, so a passing run is evidence of interoperation and not of a quiet harness."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 session interoperating with a stock eclipse-zenoh 1.0 peer on the demo topic"
      {
        h.observedWhere "controls-refused" "zenoh_stock_check" { h.atLeast "negativeControlsRefused" 138; h.atMost "negativeControlsRefused" 1000; } "The checker runs 79 mutations of an honest transcript through its own judge, 35 mutations of the corpus through its corpus judge, 19 corrupted or respelled hand-off records through the fixture contract's decoder and the exam's value checks, which read the decoded record and not the source text, three corrupted captures through its fixture check (a payload, a GID and a timestamp), and one probe of the reader that decides which retained tests passed, before it runs the Rust tests, and reports how many it refused. The bound is inclusive at 138 so a checker that drops a control is caught; the upper bound is a sanity limit, not a target.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a12f13-a343-7924-af20-18148fc1b0d9"; }; };
    (h.req "01a1b49c-abf1-7139-b388-c6f4c6b32ce9"
      "zenoh_stock_check passes as the gate adapter observes it."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 session interoperating with a stock eclipse-zenoh 1.0 peer on the demo topic"
      {
        h.observedWhere "recipe-passed" "zenoh_stock_check" h.noPredicates "The recipe's exit code, reported by the gate adapter rather than by the checker, so a checker cannot vouch for itself.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a12f13-a343-7924-af20-18148fc1b0d9"; }; };
    -- legacy 01a13943-5c10-7fbd-b1e0-a1d4c2f89f28 (done): Zenoh Profile 0 links declared by a named configuration, with fragmentation when the component supplies the storage
    -- A Zenoh Profile 0 link is sized for one message: the demo's twelve-byte Counter, one key, one
    -- subscriber, and a 512-byte batch, all fixed in code. A component that wants to exchange anything
    -- else cannot, and the bound is not the protocol's. The protocol gives a subscriber a 32-bit id, an
    -- alias a 16-bit one, and a message any length, which a peer sends as fragments when it is larger than
    -- the batch the other side announced. A stock eclipse-zenoh 1.0.0 peer was run against a logging TCP
    -- proxy and put a 5000-byte Put on the wire as three fragments of 2046, 2046 and 963 bytes at a
    -- 2048-byte batch, as ten at a 512-byte batch, and as two at 2200 bytes. Profile 0 refuses a fragment
    -- as an unsupported message, so a link that announced a larger batch would be sent fragments it cannot
    -- take, and nothing in the repository holds those bytes or states what a link may be declared with.
    (h.req "01a1b49c-ac2b-753a-83fc-23767731d9c9"
      "A configuration is judged by contracts/zenoh-link-limits/v1 before a link is built from it. The contract accepts the three pinned configurations and refuses, each on its exact boundary: a batch above one service operation (4096 accepted, 4097 refused) and a zero batch; a key count above the 16-bit alias id (65535 accepted, 65536 refused), no keys, no subscribers, more subscribers than keys, a negative key count, a zero payload and a zero key (a one-byte payload, key and sample count accepted), and a name of 16 bytes accepted and 17 refused; a key that does not fit a declaration in its batch, because the link sends a declaration whole in one frame and the longest is the key plus 18 bytes and the width of its length field, 19 to 21 with the stream length (4076 bytes accepted and 4077 refused in a 4096-byte batch, 2028 and 2029 in a 2048-byte batch, 125 in a batch of 144 accepted and 143 refused, 126 in a batch of 145 refused and 146 accepted, 70 accepted and 71 refused in the smallest batch of 89, and 65535 refused) and a payload longer than its 32-bit wire length (4294967295 accepted, 4294967296 refused); no outstanding samples and more than 1000000000, the count past which the contract's own 63-bit arithmetic would overflow (1000000000 accepted with the stack and heap it needs, 1000000001 refused, and a count of 2^62 refused and not evaluated); a stack one byte under what the link's arrays, slots and notices need and one that covers them exactly, including a link that gained keys or a longer key without more stack; a fixed payload whose frame fills the batch exactly (1869 bytes in a 2048-byte batch: the message, which is the payload, the key, the attachment, five fixed bytes and the widths of the two lengths, then a frame header and a five-byte sequence number, then the two-byte stream length) and one a byte over, ones whose message fills the batch without the frame or without the stream length reserved, which are refused, and the step in the width of the payload length at 127 and 128 bytes; a fixed link that declares a reassembly timeout or a heap; a heap link with no timeout, a negative one, no heap, a heap a byte under what every queued sample notice, two per outstanding sample, and the message being reassembled need (a heap of one message with four outstanding samples, or of two messages with one, is refused), a payload larger than its heap, and a batch below the 89 bytes the largest handshake message needs (88 refused and 89 accepted, in fixed and in heap mode, because INIT and OPEN cannot be sent as fragments); a repeated or empty name, an unknown mode and no configuration; and an attachment of any size but the 33 bytes Profile 0 requires (32, 34 and 1 refused, including a heap link certifying storage for a one-byte attachment); and a field the contract does not name or a missing one. The stack it certifies is held to the implementation's types: for each of the three configurations the Rust tests print the size of the link's arrays, slots, notices and session computed with size_of, and the judge refuses a size past the declared stack and also one past what the contract's own formula certifies for the configuration, so a layout that grew but stays under a roomy declaration is noticed, so the contract's arithmetic cannot drift from the code unnoticed. It sets no ceiling on keys, subscribers, samples, payload or heap beyond those sourced above."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 links declared by a named configuration, with fragmentation when the component supplies the storage"
      {
        h.observedWhere "cases-observed" "zenoh_links_check" { h.count "casesObserved" 286; } "The checker counts what it matched: 3 configurations, 56 scenarios, 15 stock fragments, 3 reassemblies, 3 fragments of our own Put, 2 edge lines, 2 session-sequence lines, 1 storage-reuse line, 1 declared-payload line, 1 notice-queue line, 1 aliased line, 12 deadline lines, 4 subscriber-id lines, 6 batch-edge lines, 2 fixed-edge lines, 2 minimum-configuration lines, 2 long-key alias lines, 2 single-fragment lines, 1 nine-slot line, 1 fixed nine-slot line, 6 wide-count lines, 2 slot lines, 9 peer-batch lines, 1 multibyte-key line, 1 underfilled-run line, 16 sequence-width boundary lines, 1 short-key line, 2 high-alias lines, 6 reuse-after-refusal lines, 15 width-step fragment lines, 9 declared-payload send lines, 1 drained-queue line and 3 stack-size lines, 192 positive cases, and 94 refusals, 286 in all. The count is exact rather than a floor: fewer means the Rust tests skipped a scenario, more means they reported something the exam does not name. The checker also requires every refusal class, the fragment bytes and the exact scenario names before it reports the count, so the number is only reported for a run that agreed.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a13943-5c10-7fbd-b1e0-a1d4c2f89f28"; }; };
    (h.req "01a1b49c-ac2b-753a-83fc-237727750ed9"
      "The demo configuration is exactly the first release's: a 512-byte batch, a 12-byte payload, a 129-byte key, the 33-byte attachment, one key, one subscriber, four outstanding samples and 16384 bytes of stack, in fixed mode with no timeout and no heap. The judge refuses a transcript that reports it with any field changed, and refuses the three configurations reported in another order or with one missing."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 links declared by a named configuration, with fragmentation when the component supplies the storage"
      {
        h.observedWhere "cases-observed" "zenoh_links_check" { h.count "casesObserved" 286; } "The checker counts what it matched: 3 configurations, 56 scenarios, 15 stock fragments, 3 reassemblies, 3 fragments of our own Put, 2 edge lines, 2 session-sequence lines, 1 storage-reuse line, 1 declared-payload line, 1 notice-queue line, 1 aliased line, 12 deadline lines, 4 subscriber-id lines, 6 batch-edge lines, 2 fixed-edge lines, 2 minimum-configuration lines, 2 long-key alias lines, 2 single-fragment lines, 1 nine-slot line, 1 fixed nine-slot line, 6 wide-count lines, 2 slot lines, 9 peer-batch lines, 1 multibyte-key line, 1 underfilled-run line, 16 sequence-width boundary lines, 1 short-key line, 2 high-alias lines, 6 reuse-after-refusal lines, 15 width-step fragment lines, 9 declared-payload send lines, 1 drained-queue line and 3 stack-size lines, 192 positive cases, and 94 refusals, 286 in all. The count is exact rather than a floor: fewer means the Rust tests skipped a scenario, more means they reported something the exam does not name. The checker also requires every refusal class, the fragment bytes and the exact scenario names before it reports the count, so the number is only reported for a run that agreed.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a13943-5c10-7fbd-b1e0-a1d4c2f89f28"; }; };
    (h.req "01a1b49c-ac2b-753a-83fc-237808100c30"
      "Every fragment run the stock peer wrote is taken in heap mode and reassembles to the payload that was sent: 5000 bytes at a 2048-byte batch as three fragments of 2046, 2046 and 963 bytes, 2200 bytes at 2048 as two of 2046 and 204, and 5000 bytes at 512 as ten, nine of 510 and one of 500. The headers are 0x66 on every fragment but the last, which is 0x26, and the sequence numbers are consecutive. The checker holds each encoded body to its pinned length, header and sha256, and each reassembly to the sha256 of the payload."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 links declared by a named configuration, with fragmentation when the component supplies the storage"
      {
        h.observedWhere "cases-observed" "zenoh_links_check" { h.count "casesObserved" 286; } "The checker counts what it matched: 3 configurations, 56 scenarios, 15 stock fragments, 3 reassemblies, 3 fragments of our own Put, 2 edge lines, 2 session-sequence lines, 1 storage-reuse line, 1 declared-payload line, 1 notice-queue line, 1 aliased line, 12 deadline lines, 4 subscriber-id lines, 6 batch-edge lines, 2 fixed-edge lines, 2 minimum-configuration lines, 2 long-key alias lines, 2 single-fragment lines, 1 nine-slot line, 1 fixed nine-slot line, 6 wide-count lines, 2 slot lines, 9 peer-batch lines, 1 multibyte-key line, 1 underfilled-run line, 16 sequence-width boundary lines, 1 short-key line, 2 high-alias lines, 6 reuse-after-refusal lines, 15 width-step fragment lines, 9 declared-payload send lines, 1 drained-queue line and 3 stack-size lines, 192 positive cases, and 94 refusals, 286 in all. The count is exact rather than a floor: fewer means the Rust tests skipped a scenario, more means they reported something the exam does not name. The checker also requires every refusal class, the fragment bytes and the exact scenario names before it reports the count, so the number is only reported for a run that agreed.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a13943-5c10-7fbd-b1e0-a1d4c2f89f28"; }; };
    (h.req "01a1b49c-ac2b-753a-83fc-2379ba815ac4"
      "Our own Put, which names the demo key in full where the stock peer used its alias, is split by the same rule as the stock peer's, so its bodies are the host reference's and not the captured ones: a 5000-byte payload at a 2048-byte batch with a first sequence number of 1000 gives the three bodies the reference computes, and the checker holds each to its length, header and sha256. A message travels whole in a FRAME when the frame's header, its sequence number and the message fit the batch less the stream length, and is fragmented when they do not: at a 2048-byte batch and sequence number 1000 the largest payload that travels whole is 1872 bytes, and one byte more is two fragments."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 links declared by a named configuration, with fragmentation when the component supplies the storage"
      {
        h.observedWhere "cases-observed" "zenoh_links_check" { h.count "casesObserved" 286; } "The checker counts what it matched: 3 configurations, 56 scenarios, 15 stock fragments, 3 reassemblies, 3 fragments of our own Put, 2 edge lines, 2 session-sequence lines, 1 storage-reuse line, 1 declared-payload line, 1 notice-queue line, 1 aliased line, 12 deadline lines, 4 subscriber-id lines, 6 batch-edge lines, 2 fixed-edge lines, 2 minimum-configuration lines, 2 long-key alias lines, 2 single-fragment lines, 1 nine-slot line, 1 fixed nine-slot line, 6 wide-count lines, 2 slot lines, 9 peer-batch lines, 1 multibyte-key line, 1 underfilled-run line, 16 sequence-width boundary lines, 1 short-key line, 2 high-alias lines, 6 reuse-after-refusal lines, 15 width-step fragment lines, 9 declared-payload send lines, 1 drained-queue line and 3 stack-size lines, 192 positive cases, and 94 refusals, 286 in all. The count is exact rather than a floor: fewer means the Rust tests skipped a scenario, more means they reported something the exam does not name. The checker also requires every refusal class, the fragment bytes and the exact scenario names before it reports the count, so the number is only reported for a run that agreed.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a13943-5c10-7fbd-b1e0-a1d4c2f89f28"; }; };
    (h.req "01a1b49c-ac2b-753a-83fc-237a058efc6a"
      "A fixed link refuses a fragment as unsupported-message whatever its form: a best-effort fragment, one with an extension, an empty one, and ones whose sequence number is noncanonical or above 32 bits are each exercised in fixed mode and each refused as unsupported-message, not as the class a heap link would give. It keeps every refusal and behaviour the stock-peer exam pins; the retained decoder, session, encoder and link tests and the six stock-peer exam tests (zenoh_stock::the_stock_peer_exam among them) run and pass by name beside the links tests, including the three session tests the stock-peer change added, and the stock-peer exam transcript is judged by the stock judge on this path, so deleting or renaming one of them fails the links gate and not only zenoh_stock_check. A large link delivers a 1024-byte payload whole and refuses 1025 bytes as over-bound although they would fit its batch, a demo link refuses 13 as over-bound, and a link declared with four keys and four subscribers holds them: a fifth subscriber is refused as subscriber-limit and a fifth alias as over-bound."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 links declared by a named configuration, with fragmentation when the component supplies the storage"
      {
        h.observedWhere "cases-observed" "zenoh_links_check" { h.count "casesObserved" 286; } "The checker counts what it matched: 3 configurations, 56 scenarios, 15 stock fragments, 3 reassemblies, 3 fragments of our own Put, 2 edge lines, 2 session-sequence lines, 1 storage-reuse line, 1 declared-payload line, 1 notice-queue line, 1 aliased line, 12 deadline lines, 4 subscriber-id lines, 6 batch-edge lines, 2 fixed-edge lines, 2 minimum-configuration lines, 2 long-key alias lines, 2 single-fragment lines, 1 nine-slot line, 1 fixed nine-slot line, 6 wide-count lines, 2 slot lines, 9 peer-batch lines, 1 multibyte-key line, 1 underfilled-run line, 16 sequence-width boundary lines, 1 short-key line, 2 high-alias lines, 6 reuse-after-refusal lines, 15 width-step fragment lines, 9 declared-payload send lines, 1 drained-queue line and 3 stack-size lines, 192 positive cases, and 94 refusals, 286 in all. The count is exact rather than a floor: fewer means the Rust tests skipped a scenario, more means they reported something the exam does not name. The checker also requires every refusal class, the fragment bytes and the exact scenario names before it reports the count, so the number is only reported for a run that agreed.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a13943-5c10-7fbd-b1e0-a1d4c2f89f28"; }; };
    (h.req "01a1b49c-ac2b-753a-83fc-237bcc053439"
      "In heap mode the component supplies the storage: a link built over component-supplied storage reassembles a fragmented message into it, and the library takes no allocator of its own. A message whose slices the storage cannot hold is refused as out-of-memory and leaves the session open and unchanged, a message larger than the declared heap is refused as over-bound, a gap in the fragment sequence is refused as fragment-sequence, a run whose first fragment is not the session's next expected sequence number (replayed from before it, or skipping ahead of it) is refused as sequence-mismatch and leaves the session unchanged, and a whole accepted run advances that number by the number of fragments, wrapping at 32 bits, a reassembly that does not finish within the declared timeout is refused as reassembly-timeout and releases its storage, and a whole FRAME arriving while a message is being reassembled is refused as unexpected-message. The sender splits a Put of the declared 16384 bytes body for body as the host reference does (nine fragments at a 2048-byte batch, the payload length a three-byte field), and a Put of the declared 16384 bytes reassembles into the declared heap, and one byte more is refused as over-bound even when the storage would hold it. A fragment longer than the announced batch less the two-byte stream length is refused as length-exceeds-batch. A fragmented PUSH is held to what a whole-frame one is held to and nothing more: the extension flag on the PUSH, a timestamp or an encoding on the Put, an extension that is not the attachment and a chained one are refused with the classes the corpus gives them (unsupported-extension, unsupported-message, unsupported-message, unsupported-extension, unsupported-extension), and a zero-length key as invalid-keyexpr. A fragment whose sequence number ends before it is complete is refused as truncated on a heap link and as unsupported-message on a fixed one. A configuration of nine keys, nine subscribers and nine outstanding samples is built and filled to its limits through the link, not only judged by the contract: nine keys and nine subscribers are declared, the eighteen notices of nine samples are held undrained, each a distinct payload, and one more message is reassembled, with every payload read back; the tenth subscriber is refused as subscriber-limit and the tenth alias as over-bound, so a link whose tables or queue are sized for four fails. The same nine keys, nine subscribers and nine samples are built and filled in fixed mode, where each of the eighteen notices carries a copy of its payload inline and nothing is reassembled, with the tenth subscriber refused as subscriber-limit and the tenth alias as over-bound, so a fixed link that keeps four-entry arrays fails. The counts are also taken past the widths they could be stored in, in each mode: 256 keys, 256 subscribers and 256 samples (one past what a byte holds, 512 notices held undrained, each a distinct payload that includes its own index, with the 257th subscriber refused as subscriber-limit and the 257th alias as over-bound), and 65535 keys and 65535 subscribers with one sample (the most the contract admits and all a 16-bit field holds), so a link that keeps a count in a byte or rejects its 256th slot or sample fails, and so does one that narrows a slot index below 16 bits. What a link sends is bounded by the smaller of its own batch and the batch the peer announced in its INIT, because the peer accepts no more and the link's send storage holds no more: a heap link of 4096 sends fragments of at most 87, 510 and 2046 bytes to peers announcing 89, 512 and 2048, and a link of 2048 is not lifted past its own storage by a peer announcing 4096; a fixed link, which cannot fragment, sends the largest message that fits the smaller batch whole and refuses one byte more as over-bound, a class this exam chooses where the profile names none. Our handshake and declarations cannot be fragmented either, so they must fit the peer's batch, which is checked at 89. A sender that ignores the peer's batch_size and sizes everything from its own therefore fails. A configuration of 65536 samples, one past what 16 bits hold, is built in each mode with its 131072 notices held undrained, each a distinct four-byte payload that is its own index, so a count or an index narrowed to 16 bits repeats a payload. In the 65535-slot configurations every slot is used, not only the first 256: all 65535 subscribers and all 65535 aliases are declared, a Put is carried to the subscriber and through the alias at slot 256 and at the last slot, and all 65535 subscribers are undeclared, so a link that keeps its next or free index in a byte never reaches slot 256 and fails (a key-expression alias has no undeclaration in the profile, so the aliases are declared and used only). A valid multibyte key is carried: a four-character, nine-byte key is declared and undeclared and a fragmented Put is sent and reassembled for it, and the same key with an x, five characters and ten bytes, is refused as over-bound against a declared 9, so a link that counts characters or assumes ASCII fails. A Put of 1048576 bytes (1 MiB) is sent and reassembled whole at a 4096-byte batch, far past the 16384 of the heap configuration, so a link with a hidden 16 KiB ceiling fails. Puts of 2097152 bytes (2 MiB, whose payload length is a four-byte field) and 268435456 bytes (256 MiB, the first with a five-byte field) are sent and reassembled too, so a payload-length writer that stops at three or four bytes fails. A sender may flush early, because a fragment carries at most the batch less the stream length and not exactly that: a run whose interior fragments carry 1000, 1 and 1500 bytes of slice reassembles, so a receiver that insists every fragment marked more fills the batch fails. The edge between a message that travels whole and one that is fragmented is pinned on both sides of each step in the width of the sequence number, at 127 and 128, 16383 and 16384, 2097151 and 2097152, and 268435455 and 268435456, the four steps from a one-byte sequence number to a five-byte one, where it moves by one byte, so a link that sizes the frame from a cached width sends a whole batch one byte over the announced one. A fixed frame is built at the widest sequence number, 4294967295, because the frames that fill the batch exactly only do so at that width (a frame at sequence number 1000 is three bytes shorter), so a sender or a buffer that fails only there is caught. A complete Put in a single reliable FRAGMENT with the more flag clear reassembles, at sequence number 1000 and at 4294967295, and advances the expected sequence number by one, wrapping to 0, so a receiver that insists the first fragment says more follows fails. An admitted long key is also declared as a key-expression alias, in the form that names its key in full (the encoder is shown to write the bytes the stock peer wrote), and a sender-mapped fragmented Put is resolved through it: a 2028-byte key as alias 1 at a batch of 2048, and a 4076-byte key as alias 0x8000 at a batch of 4096, so an implementation that sizes the subscriber and full-key storage to keyBytes but keeps the old 129-byte alias buffer fails. The declaration and the Put share one reliable channel, so the declaration is at sequence number 4294967294 and the fragments start at the next, 4294967295, and run across the wrap to 0 and 1, as a session that accepted the declaration expects; a run that does not start there is refused as sequence-mismatch. The reassembly timeout is varied, not only the pinned 5000 ms: heap configurations with a 250 ms, a 1 ms, a 65536 ms (past 16 bits), a 4294967296 ms (past 32 bits) and a 9223372036854775807 ms timeout are accepted by the contract, the session's clock being a 64-bit count of milliseconds, and for each the run whose last fragment arrives exactly the timeout after the first reassembles and the run one millisecond later is refused as reassembly-timeout, so a receiver that hard-codes one value expires too early or too late at the others, and one that stores a timeout in 16 or 32 bits, saturates it or rejects it expires the exact run early. The smallest configuration the contract admits is run in each mode, a one-byte key and a one-byte payload in a batch of 89, with one key, one subscriber and one sample: it is built, the key is declared and undeclared, and the Put travels whole in one frame, so a link that keeps a lower limit of three key bytes or twelve payload bytes fails. The same bounds are run in fixed mode, where nothing is fragmented and the whole Put travels in one frame: at a batch of 89 a 10-byte key is declared and a Put of 31 payload bytes, the most the contract lets the smallest batch hold, is sent whole; at a batch of 4096 a key of 1000 bytes, far past the demo's 129, is declared and a Put of 3046 payload bytes, which with its frame, built at the widest five-byte sequence number, fills the batch, is sent whole. So a fixed link that keeps the old 512 to 2048 range fails. Every edge, heap, fixed and minimum, also reports the batch its INIT_ACK announces and the batch the INIT_SYN a connector sends announces, each decoded from the handshake, and the sha256 of both, so an acceptor that announces the configured batch but a connector that announces another, which a peer would size its fragments for, fails; because the longest INIT_ACK is 87 bytes whatever batch it announces, so an encoder that announces another batch than the one configured, which a peer would size its fragments for, fails. The admitted edges run through the link, not only the contract: at a batch of 89 the INIT_ACK of 87 bytes with a 16-byte ZID and a 64-byte cookie fills the batch exactly, a 70-byte key is declared and undeclared in frames of exactly 89 bytes, and a 300-byte Put is fragmented and reassembled; at a batch of 4096 a 4076-byte key is declared and undeclared in frames of exactly 4096 bytes and a Put of 16384 bytes is fragmented and reassembled; and at a batch of 2048 a key of 2028 bytes, far past the 129 of the demo configuration, is declared, used by a fragmented 5000-byte Put and undeclared. Each reports the handshake and declaration lengths, the number of fragments, the sha256 of their bodies and the sha256 of the payload, which the checker computes from the host reference after the contract has accepted the configuration. A valid key shorter than the declared maximum reassembles (a 3-byte key that is the subscriber's own, not only the 129-byte demo key), and aliases 0x8000 and 0xffff, which have bit 15 set, are declared and resolve a sender-mapped fragmented Put. Every invalid key form the whole-frame decoder refuses is refused after reassembly: a trailing slash, a question mark and a hash as invalid-keyexpr, a dollar as wildcard-keyexpr, and a message that ends inside the bytes its key length declares as truncated. A subscriber id is any 32-bit value, zero included: ids 0, 0x7fffffff, 0x80000000 and 0xffffffff are declared and undeclared with the encodings pinned, zero being a valid subscriber id where alias scope zero is reserved and refused, so an implementation that uses zero as an empty-slot sentinel fails, so a reader or a slot of 16 or 31 bits fails. A PUSH whose body is a delete is refused as unsupported-message and one that ends before its body as truncated, and a message that ends in the middle of the scope, key length, attachment length or payload length of the reconstructed Put is refused as truncated, not defaulted. A continuation must carry the next sequence number: one that repeats the number before it or goes back is refused as fragment-sequence, as a gap is, so chunks are never joined out of order. A full key is global: one carrying a nonzero scope, which is relative to an alias, is refused as invalid-keyexpr. The more flag, not the payload length, decides where a message ends: an interior fragment that says no more follows ends the message early and is refused as truncated, and a last fragment that still says more follows leaves the run open and is refused as reassembly-timeout once the declared timeout passes with nothing further, with no sample delivered. With the eight queued sample notices of the heap configuration held and not drained, each a distinct payload of the declared 16384 bytes, a ninth message of the same size reassembles and every payload read back afterwards is the one that was sent, so none is overwritten and a partition of small slots plus one large one fails; once those samples are consumed another message of 16384 bytes reassembles into the same storage, so a link that never returns the leases of consumed samples fails. Two messages in a row reassemble independently, and a timed-out reassembly releases its storage: a message of 5000 payload bytes reassembles into storage of exactly one such message (5171 bytes) after a run that timed out in the same storage, which a buffer left leased would not allow. A fragment carries a sequence number and no message id, so what interleaving leaves behind is what is refused: fragments of two messages joined in one run are refused as trailing-bytes, and a best-effort fragment arriving during a reassembly as unsupported-message."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 links declared by a named configuration, with fragmentation when the component supplies the storage"
      {
        h.observedWhere "cases-observed" "zenoh_links_check" { h.count "casesObserved" 286; } "The checker counts what it matched: 3 configurations, 56 scenarios, 15 stock fragments, 3 reassemblies, 3 fragments of our own Put, 2 edge lines, 2 session-sequence lines, 1 storage-reuse line, 1 declared-payload line, 1 notice-queue line, 1 aliased line, 12 deadline lines, 4 subscriber-id lines, 6 batch-edge lines, 2 fixed-edge lines, 2 minimum-configuration lines, 2 long-key alias lines, 2 single-fragment lines, 1 nine-slot line, 1 fixed nine-slot line, 6 wide-count lines, 2 slot lines, 9 peer-batch lines, 1 multibyte-key line, 1 underfilled-run line, 16 sequence-width boundary lines, 1 short-key line, 2 high-alias lines, 6 reuse-after-refusal lines, 15 width-step fragment lines, 9 declared-payload send lines, 1 drained-queue line and 3 stack-size lines, 192 positive cases, and 94 refusals, 286 in all. The count is exact rather than a floor: fewer means the Rust tests skipped a scenario, more means they reported something the exam does not name. The checker also requires every refusal class, the fragment bytes and the exact scenario names before it reports the count, so the number is only reported for a run that agreed.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a13943-5c10-7fbd-b1e0-a1d4c2f89f28"; }; };
    (h.req "01a1b49c-ac2b-753a-83fc-237c5be17210"
      "A fragment that is not exactly the reliable form is refused with its class: a best-effort fragment as unsupported-message, one carrying extensions as unsupported-extension, one with no data as malformed-message, and one before the session is open as unexpected-message. A fragmented Put is held to what a single-batch Put is held to, in heap mode: without the attachment, with a 32-byte one, or with a GID length byte of 15 or 17 it is refused as malformed-message, with a 34-byte one as over-bound, with alias scope 0 as invalid-keyexpr, with an alias the session never declared (scope 2 with only alias 1 declared) as invalid-keyexpr, with a scope above 65535 as leb128-overflow, and with a 130-byte full key as over-bound (the 129-byte demo key reassembles). Every variable-length integer is read as the Rust reader reads it, on the link and not only in the host oracle: a fragment's sequence number, and the scope, the key length, the attachment length and the payload length of the reassembled Put, are each refused as leb128-noncanonical when overlong (a sequence number of 26 80 00 aa, a scope of 81 00) and as leb128-overflow when above their width (a sequence number above 32 bits, a key length above 16 bits, an attachment length or a payload length above 32 bits). An aliased Put from a peer that declared the alias is sender-mapped (header 0x5d) and reassembles, while the same Put receiver-mapped (0x1d), which names an alias this side declared and it declares none, is refused as invalid-keyexpr, and an alias that stands for a key other than the subscriber's is refused as undeclared-key. Values inside each declared width are read, not only values above it: a scope of 256, a key length of 256, an attachment length of 65536 and a payload length of 65536 are canonical in their fields and are refused for what they say (invalid-keyexpr for an alias the session never declared, over-bound for the three lengths), not as an overflow, so a reader that is too narrow fails. A payload shorter than its length says is refused as truncated. A reassembly is measured from its first fragment: it completes when the last arrives exactly the declared timeout (5000 ms) after it, and the same run one millisecond later is refused as reassembly-timeout, both pinned with a first timestamp of 7000000 ms, long after the clock passed the timeout, and a middle fragment one millisecond before the edge, so a deadline compared with the absolute clock and one refreshed by each continuation both fail, the rule the session's lease uses, so a timeout shorter than declared fails and so does a longer one. The top bit of each width is read too, so a reader one bit narrow fails: a scope of 32768 and a key length of 32768 (canonical in 16 bits), and an attachment length and a payload length of 2147483648 (canonical in 32), are refused for what they say (invalid-keyexpr for the undeclared alias, over-bound for the three lengths), not as overflows. A message that ends inside its 33-byte attachment is refused as truncated. A Put split across a sequence-number width change is pinned body for body against the host reference, one run from each step of the encoding (127, 16383, 2097151 and 268435455) and one across the 32-bit wrap (4294967294, whose headers shrink from five bytes to one), so a link that sizes every slice from its first fragment's header and overruns the announced batch fails. After each way a reassembly can end without delivering a sample, a sequence gap, a whole FRAME in the middle, a best-effort fragment in the middle, and a completed message refused for a malformed Put, a key the subscriber did not declare or a payload over the declared size, a message of 5000 payload bytes reassembles into storage of exactly one message, so a refusal that leaves the buffer leased fails. A full key is held to what a whole-frame key is held to: one with a wildcard is refused as wildcard-keyexpr, one with a leading or doubled slash or that is not UTF-8 as invalid-keyexpr, and a well-formed key other than the subscriber's as undeclared-key, so a reassembled Put cannot reach a topic the subscriber did not declare. The classes are the checker's, not the implementation's, and a fixed link refuses every fragment as unsupported-message whatever its form."
      "Zenoh Profile 0 links declared by a named configuration, with fragmentation when the component supplies the storage"
      {
        h.observedWhere "cases-observed" "zenoh_links_check" { h.count "casesObserved" 286; } "The checker counts what it matched: 3 configurations, 56 scenarios, 15 stock fragments, 3 reassemblies, 3 fragments of our own Put, 2 edge lines, 2 session-sequence lines, 1 storage-reuse line, 1 declared-payload line, 1 notice-queue line, 1 aliased line, 12 deadline lines, 4 subscriber-id lines, 6 batch-edge lines, 2 fixed-edge lines, 2 minimum-configuration lines, 2 long-key alias lines, 2 single-fragment lines, 1 nine-slot line, 1 fixed nine-slot line, 6 wide-count lines, 2 slot lines, 9 peer-batch lines, 1 multibyte-key line, 1 underfilled-run line, 16 sequence-width boundary lines, 1 short-key line, 2 high-alias lines, 6 reuse-after-refusal lines, 15 width-step fragment lines, 9 declared-payload send lines, 1 drained-queue line and 3 stack-size lines, 192 positive cases, and 94 refusals, 286 in all. The count is exact rather than a floor: fewer means the Rust tests skipped a scenario, more means they reported something the exam does not name. The checker also requires every refusal class, the fragment bytes and the exact scenario names before it reports the count, so the number is only reported for a run that agreed.";
      }) with { supersedes = { "01a13943-5c10-7fbd-b1e0-a1d4c2f89f28"; }; };
    (h.req "01a1b49c-ac2b-753a-83fc-237d6cf8ac6d"
      "The stock-peer exam's corpus judge lets the transport_fragment row, the single reliable final fragment 2601aa, leave vectors.txt and, while it exists, requires its captured bytes and its class unsupported-message; it refuses the row turned into an accepted row, with other bytes, with another existing class and with an invented class. The 100 other refused vectors keep their name, class and bytes under a recomputed digest, and the 17 accepted rows and 14 stream rows are unchanged."
